Chebyshev's own constants were sharper. Working with the combination $\lfloor x\rfloor - \lfloor x/2\rfloor - \lfloor x/3\rfloor - \lfloor x/5\rfloor + \lfloor x/30\rfloor$ in place of central binomial coefficients, he showed that for all sufficiently large $x$,
$$0.92129\,x < \psi(x) < 1.10555\,x.$$
He also proved that if $\psi(x)/x$ (equivalently $\pi(x)\log x/x$) has a limit, the limit must be $1$. Proving that the limit exists took nearly another half-century and the methods of complex analysis. Numerically the ratio is already close to $1$:
- $\psi(10^2) \approx 94.05$, ratio $0.9405$;
- $\psi(10^3) \approx 996.68$, ratio $0.9967$;
- $\psi(10^4) \approx 10013.40$, ratio $1.0013$;
- $\psi(10^6) \approx 999586.6$, ratio $0.99959$.

The fluctuations of $\psi(x) - x$, visible here as changes of sign, are governed by the zeros of $\zeta$: this is the content of the [[explicit-formula]], proved by von Mangoldt in 1895.
